  "summary": "A slotted rotor turns off-centre inside a cylindrical liner (cam). Flat vanes slide in and out of the rotor slots, pressed against the liner by centrifugal force (and often push rods or springs), so they form sealed chambers between rotor and liner. On the inlet side the chambers grow and fill; on the outlet side they shrink and push the liquid out. Vanes wear-compensate by sliding further out, keeping performance up on thin liquids such as LPG, fuels, solvents and refrigerants.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Positive displacement – rotary",
    "configuration": "Sliding vane, eccentric rotor, single chamber (unbalanced)"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "vanes": 6,
    "eccentricity_mm": 11.0,
    "vane_material": "Carbon-graphite/composite (typical); metal for high viscosity",
    "note": "Balanced (double-cam) designs exist for hydraulic service."
  },
